<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-matrix-multiplication" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Matrix Multiplication</title>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       INTRO
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <p>
    The last operation we need is multiplying two matrices together. Unlike with matrix addition, you can't just
    multiply component-wise. The definition builds off of the matrix-vector product.
  </p>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       DEFINITION: MATRIX MULTIPLICATION
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <definition xml:id="def-matrix-multiplication">
    <title>Matrix Multiplication</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Suppose <m>B</m> is an <m>m \times p</m> matrix and <m>A</m> is a <m>p \times n</m>
        matrix. Then <m>BA</m> is an <m>m \times n</m> matrix where:
        <me>BA = \begin{bmatrix} B\vec{a}_1 \amp \cdots \amp B\vec{a}_n \end{bmatrix},</me>
        where <m>\vec{a}_i</m> is the <m>i</m>th <em>column</em> of <m>A</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <p>
    In practice, we compute the entries of <m>BA</m> one at a time using dot products:
    <me>(BA)_{ij} = (i\text{th row of } B) \cdot (j\text{th col of } A).</me>
    So just like the matrix-vector product, we use the <alert>rows of <m>B</m></alert> and the
    <alert>columns of <m>A</m></alert>.
  </p>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       WORKED EXAMPLE (PROSE)
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <p>
    For example, let's multiply a <m>2 \times 3</m> matrix by a <m>3 \times 3</m> matrix
    (sizes match: <m>3</m> columns on the left, <m>3</m> rows on the right, so the result
    is <m>2 \times 3</m>). Writing <m>\vec{b}_1, \vec{b}_2</m> for the rows of the left matrix
    and <m>\vec{a}_1, \vec{a}_2, \vec{a}_3</m> for the columns of the right matrix:
   <md>
      <mrow>\begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp -1 \amp 0 \\ 2 \amp 0 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}
        \begin{bmatrix} 3 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 5 \amp 0 \\ 2 \amp -2 \amp 0 \end{bmatrix}
        \amp= \begin{bmatrix}
          \vec{b}_1 \cdot \vec{a}_1 \amp \vec{b}_1 \cdot \vec{a}_2 \amp \vec{b}_1 \cdot \vec{a}_3 \\
          \vec{b}_2 \cdot \vec{a}_1 \amp \vec{b}_2 \cdot \vec{a}_2 \amp \vec{b}_2 \cdot \vec{a}_3
        \end{bmatrix}</mrow>
      <mrow>\amp= \begin{bmatrix}
          (1,-1,0) \cdot (3,0,2) \amp (1,-1,0) \cdot (1,5,-2) \amp (1,-1,0) \cdot (0,0,0) \\
          (2,0,1) \cdot (3,0,2) \amp (2,0,1) \cdot (1,5,-2) \amp (2,0,1) \cdot (0,0,0)
        \end{bmatrix}</mrow>
      <mrow>\amp= \begin{bmatrix}
          1 \cdot 3 - 1 \cdot 0 + 0 \cdot 2 \amp 1 \cdot 1 - 1 \cdot 5 + 0 \cdot (-2) \amp 0 + 0 + 0 \\
          2 \cdot 3 + 0 \cdot 0 + 1 \cdot 2 \amp 2 \cdot 1 + 0 \cdot 5 + 1 \cdot (-2) \amp 0 + 0 + 0
        \end{bmatrix}</mrow>
      <mrow>\amp= \begin{bmatrix} 3 \amp -4 \amp 0 \\ 8 \amp -1 \amp 0 \end{bmatrix}.</mrow>
    </md>
    Each entry is one dot product: for instance, the entry in the first row, second column
    came from dotting the first row of the left matrix (<m>\vec{b}_1</m>) with the second
    column of the right matrix (<m>\vec{a}_2</m>).
  </p>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       ASSOCIATIVE BUT NOT COMMUTATIVE
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <p>
    Matrix multiplication is <alert>associative</alert>, but it is <alert>not commutative</alert>.
    Associative means we can move parentheses around:
    <me>ABC = A(BC) = (AB)C.</me>
    Not commutative means that in general,
    <me>BA \neq AB.</me>
  </p>

  <p>
    Sometimes the product in the reversed order isn't even defined!
    Take the two matrices from the example above. We computed <m>BA</m> where <m>B</m> was
    <m>2 \times 3</m> and <m>A</m> was <m>3 \times 3</m>. But if we try the other order,
    <m>AB</m>, we would be multiplying a <m>3 \times 3</m> matrix by a <m>2 \times 3</m>
    matrix. Now the number of columns of the first matrix (<m>3</m>) does not match the number
    of rows of the second (<m>2</m>), so <m>AB</m> is <alert>not defined</alert>.
  </p>

  <p>
    Even when both orders <em>are</em> defined, we still don't have commutativity. For example:
    <me>\begin{bmatrix} 0 \amp -1 \\ 1 \amp 0 \end{bmatrix}
      \begin{bmatrix} -1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}
      = \begin{bmatrix} 0 \amp -1 \\ -1 \amp 0 \end{bmatrix},</me>
    but multiplying in the other order gives
    <me>\begin{bmatrix} -1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}
      \begin{bmatrix} 0 \amp -1 \\ 1 \amp 0 \end{bmatrix}
      = \begin{bmatrix} 0 \amp 1 \\ 1 \amp 0 \end{bmatrix}.</me>
    Both products are <m>2 \times 2</m>, but they are not equal! So be careful:
    the order of multiplication matters with matrices.
  </p>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       VIDEO + QUESTION FORM
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

